  abstract = {This paper explores the complexity of deep feedforward networks with linear pre-synaptic couplings and rectified linear activations. This is a contribution to the growing body of work contrasting the representational power of deep and shallow network architectures. In particular, we offer a framework for comparing deep and shallow models that belong to the family of piecewise linear functions based on computational geometry. We look at a deep rectifier multi-layer perceptron (MLP) with linear outputs units and compare it with a single layer version of the model. In the asymptotic regime, when the number of inputs stays constant, if the shallow model has \$kn\$ hidden units and \$n\_0\$ inputs, then the number of linear regions is \$O(k\textasciicircum\textbraceleft n\_0\textbraceright n\textasciicircum\textbraceleft n\_0\textbraceright )\$. For a \$k\$ layer model with \$n\$ hidden units on each layer it is \$\textbackslash Omega(\textbackslash left\textbackslash lfloor \textbraceleft n\textbraceright/\textbraceleft n\_0\textbraceright\textbackslash right\textbackslash rfloor\textasciicircum\textbraceleft k-1\textbraceright n\textasciicircum\textbraceleft n\_0\textbraceright )\$. The number \$\textbackslash left\textbackslash lfloor\textbraceleft n\textbraceright/\textbraceleft n\_0\textbraceright\textbackslash right\textbackslash rfloor\textasciicircum\textbraceleft k-1\textbraceright\$ grows faster than \$k\textasciicircum\textbraceleft n\_0\textbraceright\$ when \$n\$ tends to infinity or when \$k\$ tends to infinity and \$n \textbackslash geq 2n\_0\$. Additionally, even when \$k\$ is small, if we restrict \$n\$ to be \$2n\_0\$, we can show that a deep model has considerably more linear regions that a shallow one. We consider this as a first step towards understanding the complexity of these models and specifically towards providing suitable mathematical tools for future analysis.},
